  (61, $q$The deeper that sorrow carves into your being, the more joy you can contain.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Kahlil Gibran$q$, $q$The Prophet$q$, $q$“On Joy and Sorrow”$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/58585$q$, false, 17, 18, null, array[$q$love$q$, $q$calm$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 58585, null, $q$7247a9b08297d7fc029b6a6ded2beb0f61693eef4bfa7193f399b2c88cce89d4$q$, 19867, 19943, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (62, $q$Let life be beautiful like summer flowers and death like autumn leaves.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Rabindranath Tagore$q$, $q$Stray Birds$q$, $q$Stray Birds (1916), no. 82$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6524$q$, false, 9, 10, null, array[$q$time$q$, $q$nature$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 6524, null, $q$f589a830a307b6844504e6b03b764854b09352f05ff2c6db291cc9d7dc8ab7cb$q$, 7308, 7379, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (63, $q$If you have built castles in the air, your work need not be lost; that is where they should be. Now put the foundations under them.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Henry David Thoreau$q$, $q$Walden$q$, $q$Conclusion$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/205$q$, false, 8, 8, null, array[$q$purpose$q$, $q$courage$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 205, null, $q$9694e150f384e231fab223599597c6d3c8a95688ea591c335d454cc6e8990cec$q$, 565642, 565773, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (64, $q$Your children are not your children.
They are the sons and daughters of Life’s longing for itself.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Kahlil Gibran$q$, $q$The Prophet$q$, $q$“On Children”$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/58585$q$, false, 17, 18, null, array[$q$love$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 58585, null, $q$7247a9b08297d7fc029b6a6ded2beb0f61693eef4bfa7193f399b2c88cce89d4$q$, 11276, 11375, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (65, $q$If a man does not keep pace with his companions, perhaps it is because he hears a different drummer. Let him step to the music which he hears, however measured or far away.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Henry David Thoreau$q$, $q$Walden$q$, $q$Conclusion$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/205$q$, false, 8, 8, null, array[$q$self$q$, $q$courage$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 205, null, $q$9694e150f384e231fab223599597c6d3c8a95688ea591c335d454cc6e8990cec$q$, 569310, 569482, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (66, $q$Every child comes with the message that God is not yet discouraged of man.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Rabindranath Tagore$q$, $q$Stray Birds$q$, $q$Stray Birds (1916), no. 77$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6524$q$, false, 9, 10, null, array[$q$love$q$, $q$purpose$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 6524, null, $q$f589a830a307b6844504e6b03b764854b09352f05ff2c6db291cc9d7dc8ab7cb$q$, 6928, 7002, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (67, $q$Any man’s death diminishes me, because I am involved in mankind, and therefore never send to know for whom the bell tolls; it tolls for thee.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$John Donne$q$, $q$Devotions upon Emergent Occasions$q$, $q$Meditation XVII$q$, $q$https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Devotions/Meditation_17$q$, false, 60, 60, null, array[$q$love$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, null, 13696894, $q$7b24e19490af87aa34807a41b374ace4dfe925f2e7535caa9d1418497066f520$q$, 2628, 2769, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (68, $q$A friend is a person with whom I may be sincere. Before him I may think aloud.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Ralph Waldo Emerson$q$, $q$Friendship$q$, $q$“Friendship”, Essays: First Series (1841)$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2944$q$, false, 1, 71, null, array[$q$love$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 2944, null, $q$b5e2d54d63f99e855fc4726322f2a6520288cfeb32c0cbd5c9c8a9d7b0b08dd1$q$, 228228, 228306, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (69, $q$Do not act as if thou wert going to live ten thousand years. Death hangs over thee. While thou livest, while it is in thy power, be good.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Marcus Aurelius$q$, $q$Meditations (trans. George Long)$q$, $q$Book IV, §17$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15877$q$, false, 11, 12, null, array[$q$time$q$, $q$purpose$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 15877, null, $q$c94855366014552f7d493b94db0c6484a36408179069fa54871d4c3a87f8dc75$q$, 185351, 185488, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (70, $q$Hatred does not cease by hatred at any time: hatred ceases by love, this is an old rule.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$The Buddha$q$, $q$Dhammapada (trans. F. Max Müller)$q$, $q$Chapter I (The Twin-Verses), verse 5$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2017$q$, false, 32, 32, null, array[$q$love$q$, $q$calm$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 2017, null, $q$a08b9e2b6834a4c786711605f6da4973e956301f778c7a9c41704d09e19d8a34$q$, 1893, 1981, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (71, $q$When I play with my cat who knows whether I do not make her more sport than she makes me?$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Michel de Montaigne$q$, $q$Apology for Raimond Sebond (trans. Charles Cotton)$q$, $q$Essays, Book II, ch. XII, “Apology for Raimond Sebond”$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3600$q$, false, 35, 83, null, array[$q$nature$q$, $q$self$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 3600, null, $q$bde3d40574c053bd6f44c4136e9269aeb5470d9e7026e35de9bfced800789da1$q$, 1230875, 1230964, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (72, $q$The growing good of the world is partly dependent on unhistoric acts; and that things are not so ill with you and me as they might have been, is half owing to the number who lived faithfully a hidden life, and rest in unvisited tombs.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$George Eliot$q$, $q$Middlemarch$q$, $q$Finale, last sentence$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/145$q$, false, 19, 20, null, array[$q$purpose$q$, $q$love$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 145, null, $q$3f7f17746cb8400b5241d37f799bdfc867a0b35b7bb0ae4757b8f1ceda78ba05$q$, 1779938, 1780172, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (73, $q$Do you think, because I am poor, obscure, plain, and little, I am soulless and heartless? You think wrong!$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Charlotte Brontë$q$, $q$Jane Eyre$q$, $q$Chapter 23, Jane to Rochester$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1260$q$, false, 12, 13, $q$Jane$q$, array[$q$self$q$, $q$courage$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 1260, null, $q$1c2bd174cfa83d2f9603a8ce8c4186402eed3ea0ea39bfd534f9d67ccd0aa0fb$q$, 567769, 567876, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (74, $q$We wear the mask that grins and lies,
It hides our cheeks and shades our eyes,—
This debt we pay to human guile.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Paul Laurence Dunbar$q$, $q$We Wear the Mask$q$, $q$“We Wear the Mask”, lines 1–3$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18338$q$, false, 27, 81, null, array[$q$self$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 18338, null, $q$2a4d9b93247a4ef21b1067471ad93e7984d73ea74940af6c3c9cdccec769509e$q$, 181234, 181351, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (75, $q$The hour of departure has arrived, and we go our ways—I to die, and you to live. Which is better God only knows.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Socrates$q$, $q$Plato’s Apology (trans. Benjamin Jowett)$q$, $q$Apology 42a, Socrates’ last words to the jury$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1656$q$, false, 28, 29, null, array[$q$time$q$, $q$courage$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 1656, null, $q$f1db65c8de95a3d3cb182c126d59939213b1c02195a9281efada098070e06362$q$, 85684, 85796, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (76, $q$I’m so glad I live in a world where there are Octobers.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$L. M. Montgomery$q$, $q$Anne of Green Gables$q$, $q$Chapter XVI, Anne$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45$q$, false, 10, 11, $q$Anne$q$, array[$q$nature$q$, $q$time$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 45, null, $q$99e1d25aa5ba87a999d5938743520983efa8e5c210bdbd6b5cd5a0320d79afe8$q$, 216508, 216563, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (77, $q$Let us endeavor so to live that when we come to die even the undertaker will be sorry.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Mark Twain$q$, $q$Pudd’nhead Wilson$q$, $q$Chapter 6 epigraph, “Pudd’nhead Wilson’s Calendar”$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/102$q$, false, 14, 15, null, array[$q$purpose$q$, $q$time$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 102, null, $q$f737a722b79f96da2d1d10db5aecbdce5fef8d027adbd8826ebd6944e969028b$q$, 63999, 64087, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (78, $q$Nothing can bring you peace but yourself. Nothing can bring you peace but the triumph of principles.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Ralph Waldo Emerson$q$, $q$Self-Reliance$q$, $q$“Self-Reliance”, Essays: First Series (1841), last lines$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2944$q$, false, 1, 1, null, array[$q$calm$q$, $q$self$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 2944, null, $q$b5e2d54d63f99e855fc4726322f2a6520288cfeb32c0cbd5c9c8a9d7b0b08dd1$q$, 102328, 102428, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (79, $q$Even if the doctor does not give you a year, even if he hesitates about a month, make one brave push and see what can be accomplished in a week.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Robert Louis Stevenson$q$, $q$Æs Triplex$q$, $q$“Æs Triplex”, Virginibus Puerisque (1881)$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/386$q$, false, 23, 85, null, array[$q$courage$q$, $q$time$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 386, null, $q$06d877a0afe5155d4fb8f9b9227c51851b0b13f5f87b0ae77e42ebcaf659291f$q$, 169642, 169786, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (80, $q$I would permit no man, no matter what his colour might be, to narrow and degrade my soul by making me hate him.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$Booker T. Washington$q$, $q$Up from Slavery$q$, $q$Chapter VII, “Early Days at Tuskegee”$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2376$q$, false, 54, 54, null, array[$q$self$q$, $q$courage$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 2376, null, $q$a2886effe1d829ce9e3e23fade780c811d0d8f82a1a75594d33f2c6b937fe878$q$, 223102, 223213, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (81, $q$For oft when on my couch I lie
In vacant or in pensive mood,
They flash upon that inward eye
Which is the bliss of solitude,
And then my heart with pleasure fills,
And dances with the Daffodils.$q$, '[]'::jsonb, $q$William Wordsworth$q$, $q$I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud$q$, $q$“I wandered lonely as a Cloud”, last stanza$q$, $q$https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8824$q$, false, 58, 75, null, array[$q$nature$q$, $q$calm$q$]::text[], $q$live$q$, 8824, null, $q$d15107c0717ea57b366497bf8c525e17d9effbb7e4ec83c6c75b9df8923c526e$q$, 26211, 26415, $q$exact$q$, '2026-10-29T01:59:19Z'::timestamptz, $q$5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5$q$),
  (82, $q$What though the radiance which was once so bright
Be now for ever taken from my sight,
Though nothing can bring back the hour
